 "result": "163 tests ran: 161 passed, 1 failed and 1 errored. All 160 `test_many` cases and `test_no_discount` passed.\n\n**1. `test_parse_amount` (`tests/test_parse.py:7`): ERROR**\n```\nValueError: could not convert string to float: '1,234.50'\n```\nThe test calls `parse_amount(\"1,234.50\")` and expects `1234.5`. `calc/parse.py:5` runs `float(text.strip())`, and `float()` can't parse the thousands-separator comma, so it raises before the assertion is checked.\n\n**2. `test_discount_rounding` (`tests/test_pricing.py:7`): FAIL**\n```\nAssertionError: 1.14 != 1.15\n```\n`apply_discount(2.30, 50)` returned `1.14` where the test expects `1.15`. I didn't open the pricing code. The likely cause is floating-point rounding: 2.30 \u00d7 0.5 comes out as about 1.1499999\u2026, which rounds down to 1.14.\n\nI haven't changed anything. I can fix either one if you want.",
